<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- agent-infra-plist-version: 0.1.0 -->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- worktree reaper (#1095, armed via #1311): a daily pass over the
         tortoise repo's worktrees. The reaper itself is
         agent-infra/scripts/pi-reap-worktrees.sh, farmed to
         {{HOME}}/.pi/agent/scripts/ by pi-bootstrap/setup.sh's fleet-scripts
         farm.

         WHY THE ENTRY POINT IS THE CHECKOUT'S OWN PYTHON, NOT `bash`.
         macOS TCC is per-binary and denies a launchd-spawned `bash` the read of
         a script under ~/Documents ("Operation not permitted") AND a
         launchd-spawned `git` the read of the repo there ("not a git
         repository"). The reaper's whole job is `git -C <repo> ...` against
         that checkout, so a `bash <script>` entry point cannot work no matter
         where the script lives: farming it out of ~/Documents fixes the script
         read and nothing else. The binary that DOES hold the access is the
         checkout's own interpreter ({{TORTOISE_REPO}}/.venv/bin/python), the
         same one the already-working com.tortoise.embedded-reaper agent runs.
         So launchd starts that interpreter on
         pi-reap-worktrees-launchd.py, which reads the script itself and hands
         it to bash on stdin; bash and the `git` it spawns inherit the access
         from that process. This mirrors the configuration recorded as PROVEN
         end-to-end (WORKTREES=454 ... GH=ok), not the falsified `bash` one.

         STILL SHIPS DRY-RUN — DELIBERATELY, AND THE RULING IS #1678.
         "The reaper may run unattended; arming it may not" (owner ruling
         2026-10-11). Do NOT commit a literal `apply` flag here: install-launchd.sh
         with no arguments installs EVERY template and pi-bootstrap/setup.sh
         runs exactly that on every Darwin box, so a literal in a shared template
         arms a fleet with no human step. Arming is a separate, dated, reversible
         step.

         WHERE THE ARMING ACTUALLY IS — two different jobs, and pointing at the
         wrong one wastes the operator's afternoon. The supported, recorded verb
         is `install-launchd.sh arm-worktree-hygiene`, and it arms the SIBLING
         unit com.tortoise.worktree-hygiene (its record is
         state/worktree-hygiene.armed, its placeholder is WORKTREE_HYGIENE_MODE),
         whose sweep runs `scripts/pi-reap-worktrees.sh apply` — so that verb does
         give a box an ARMED reaper. It does NOT arm THIS unit: this template
         carries no placeholder and no arming verb exists for it, so arming this
         job is a hand-edit of the flag in ProgramArguments below plus a re-run of
         scripts/install-launchd.sh, which #1678 requires to stay a separate,
         dated, reversible step rather than something a template does by itself.
         ⛔ Do NOT write the placeholder spelling with braces in this comment:
         install-launchd.sh's render() substitutes over the WHOLE file, comments
         included, so a placeholder in a comment renders to a value below that
         starts with two hyphens, and an XML comment may not contain one
         (W3C XML 1.0 §2.5 — the sibling's WORKTREE_HYGIENE_MODE placeholder,
         spelled here WITHOUT braces on purpose, is exactly that value).
         Verified: python plistlib raises ExpatError on that render in BOTH modes;
         `plutil` tolerates it, which is exactly why the leak would ship unnoticed.
         Removing the flag is also NOT arming: with no mode flag the reaper
         falls back to REAP_WT_DRY_RUN, whose default is 1.
         ⚠️ FLAG NAMES ARE SPELLED WITHOUT THEIR LEADING DASHES THROUGHOUT THIS
         COMMENT, as in the sibling com.tortoise.worktree-hygiene.plist. An XML
         comment may not contain a double hyphen (the only legal occurrence is
         its own closing delimiter), so a literal flag spelling here makes the
         template unparseable: plutil/launchctl reject the unit, and
         install-launchd.sh's plist_program_args swallows the parse error, so the
         job would install with EMPTY program arguments instead of failing loudly.
         Keep the prose, keep the dashes out (#2041 review).

         MEASURED 2026-10-22 — why this job reported exit 0 and reclaimed
         nothing for months. 748 worktrees (551 tortoise + 197 agent-infra),
         load 222, `fseventsd` 100% CPU, ~150 MB free; last pass
         `WORKTREES=538 REMOVE=7 REMOVED=0 DEFERRED=422`. THREE causes:
           1. the installed unit ran the `dry-run` flag, so REMOVED=0 was structural;
           2. the FARMED script was ~190 diff lines BEHIND `origin/main` — the
              merged-age exemption had shipped but was never deployed, so the
              box ran rules that held every merged worktree for 7 days. (The
              farm also omitted `lib/bounded.sh`, which the newer reaper
              requires: deploying it un-fixed would have armed a job that
              FAIL-CLOSED exited 3 on every pass.)
           3. a budget-limited pass classified the HEAD of a stable-ordered list
              and deferred the same TAIL, so the newest worktrees — the ones a
              pass exists to reap — were never reached at all.

         The pass is bounded by REAP_WT_BUDGET_SECONDS; a pass that exhausts it
         defers the remainder and reports DEFERRED rather than removing anything
         unclassified. The budget is also the lever deciding how much one pass
         can classify.

         BUDGET, RAISED 2026-10-11 — 300s was measured TOO SMALL for the live
         estate: a pass over 454 worktrees spent the whole budget and reported
         DEFERRED=432 (only 22 classified), and 294 worktrees still deferred 65.
         The budget bounds classification AND removal per worktree, so at 300s
         the job could never reach the rows it exists to reap. Raised in two
         steps — to 1800s, then to the 3600s shipped in REAP_WT_BUDGET_SECONDS
         below (quoted here rather than named, because a stale number in an
         operator-facing comment is read as the shipped value). Whatever value
         is set there, REAP_WT_LOCK_STALE_SECONDS below must keep exceeding it:
         the lock's staleness test is an AGE test that ignores the budget.

         GH TIMEOUT, 2026-10-11 — the job fail-closed with
         `GH pr-list failed (rc=124) ... exit 3` on two passes (WORKTREES=388
         and 259, PRESERVE=0), i.e. `gh pr list` was KILLED BY THE 20s DEFAULT
         `REAP_WT_GH_TIMEOUT` on a loaded box (rc=124 is the watchdog's timeout
         code; it is NOT a missing or unresolvable binary). PATH below already
         resolves `gh` to the real Mach-O at {{HOME}}/bin/gh — verified — so the
         fix is time, not location. Raised to 120s below.

         The repo argument is {{TORTOISE_REPO}}. A machine without a tortoise
         checkout leaves that placeholder unresolved, and install-launchd.sh
         then SKIPS this template by design (unresolved placeholder) instead of
         installing a broken target.

         OWNERSHIP: this label is the one the job was originally armed under by
         hand. Installing this template therefore REPLACES that hand-made agent
         with the tracked one: same interpreter, same launcher, now reproducible
         from source. That replacement is the point, not a collision.

         No StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath: every pass writes its own capped
         log at ~/.pi/agent/state/pi-reap-worktrees.log (an unbounded
         launchd-captured log is the twin of the symlink-truncation surface the
         reaper already guards). -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.tortoise.worktree-reaper</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>{{TORTOISE_REPO}}/.venv/bin/python</string>
        <string>{{HOME}}/.pi/agent/scripts/pi-reap-worktrees-launchd.py</string>
        <string>--script</string>
        <string>{{HOME}}/.pi/agent/scripts/pi-reap-worktrees.sh</string>
        <string>--repo</string>
        <string>{{TORTOISE_REPO}}</string>
        <string>--dry-run</string>
    </array>
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>{{PATH}}</string>
        <!-- Both bounds are IN THE PLIST, not inherited from the operator's
             shell: a launchd job gets neither the shell's env nor its rc files,
             so an unset variable here silently keeps the script's 300s/20s
             defaults — which are exactly the two values measured to make this
             job a no-op (DEFERRED=432, and the rc=124 fail-closed abort). -->
        <key>REAP_WT_BUDGET_SECONDS</key>
        <string>3600</string>
        <key>REAP_WT_GH_TIMEOUT</key>
        <string>120</string>
        <!-- The lock's staleness horizon MUST exceed the pass budget, or a
             LEGITIMATELY-RUNNING armed pass trips its own staleness mid-pass and
             a second invocation breaks the lock and runs concurrently. Two
             concurrent armed passes each compute PRUNE_BLOCKED for their own
             pass only, so a deferred (UNCLASSIFIED) prunable record in pass A is
             invisible to pass B, which then runs the path-filterless
             `git worktree prune` — the F17/A15 P0. 14400s = 4x the budget. -->
        <key>REAP_WT_LOCK_STALE_SECONDS</key>
        <string>14400</string>
    </dict>
    <!-- LowPriorityIO: this job's work IS bulk deletion (hundreds of checkouts,
         millions of files), and at normal IO priority it competes with the very
         thing it exists to relieve — `fseventsd` and the operator's foreground
         work. `Nice` is deliberately NOT set: the budget, not a CPU penalty,
         is what bounds the pass, and a niced pass would simply defer more rows.
         The rotation below means a deferred tail is still reached next pass. -->
    <key>LowPriorityIO</key>
    <true/>
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <!-- 21600s = 6h. Measured growth: 194 -> 259 -> 294 worktrees across a few
         passes, i.e. DAILY was too rare for the estate to stop growing, and it
         is why the count reached 294 before the removal path was ever usable. -->
    <integer>21600</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
